th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/level-set-and-level-curve.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/level-set-and-level-curve.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the level set $L_k(f)$ of a real-valued function $f$?","options":["$f(\\{k\\})$","$\\{k\\}$ only","$\\mathbb{R}^n\\setminus\\{k\\}$","$f^{-1}(\\{k\\})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b7dc15078a2e6e8e7ccab425","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which claim about level sets of functions $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is false?","options":["For some $f$ and $k$, $L_k(f)$ is a circle.","For every $f$ and $k$, $L_k(f)\\neq L_{k+1}(f)$.","For every $f$ and $k$, $L_k(f)\\cap L_{k+1}(f)=\\varnothing$.","For some $f$ and $k$, $L_k(f)=\\mathbb{R}^2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"05ecf9ffce506370f767b225","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$, what is $L_0(f)$?","options":["The empty set","A circle of positive radius","$\\mathbb{R}^2$","$\\{(0,0)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e7b347ab731149edcda08435","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the union of level sets over all possible values cover?","options":["The range of the function, as a subset of $\\mathbb{R}$.","The whole of $\\mathbb{R}^n$, whatever the domain of the function.","The entire domain where the function is defined.","The graph of the function, as a subset of $\\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4972fa810d79c0014587a5a6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $(2,1)\\in L_k(f)$ for $f(x,y)=x+y$, which statement is the safest conclusion?","options":["$k=3$, and the level set $L_3(f)$ is just the single point $(2,1)$.","$k=3$, and $L_3(f)$ is the whole line $x+y=3$, not only the point $(2,1)$.","$k$ is the point $(2,1)$ itself, since level sets are indexed by points.","$k=3$, and $L_3(f)$ is the line $y=x-1$ through the point $(2,1)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7b741f94be0511ab85d0ffd0","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x+y$, which equation describes the level set $L_3(f)$?","options":["$x+y=0$","$x+y=3$","$x-y=3$","$xy=3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3f81695b174121468fd9fe7e","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$, what is $L_{-1}(f)$?","options":["The unit circle","$\\{(0,0)\\}$","$\\varnothing$","The line $x+y=-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4764e26f18f04dd622444a49","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$, which level set is the unit circle?","options":["$L_0(f)$","$L_{-1}(f)$","$L_2(f)$","$L_1(f)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1033a7a497b77668246d83b2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_014_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}$ and $k\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The level curve $L_k(f)$ is a subset of which set?","options":["The domain $\\mathbb{R}^2$ of the inputs $(x,y)$.","The graph of $f$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","The codomain $\\mathbb{R}$ of the values.","The horizontal plane $z=k$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac9970c07a7ebe7da1b8fcfa","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_019_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $a\\neq b$ be real numbers. Which statement about $L_a(f)$ and $L_b(f)$ is always true?","options":["$L_a(f)\\cap L_b(f)=\\varnothing$.","$L_a(f)\\cup L_b(f)=X$.","$L_a(f)$ and $L_b(f)$ are both nonempty.","$L_a(f)\\cap L_b(f)$ consists of exactly one point."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"207fa79baa70a8401ba39778","time_limit":30}]}}